  "explanation": "Modern computer systems utilize a memory hierarchy to bridge the speed gap between CPU and main memory. This hierarchy consists of multiple levels of storage with varying speeds and capacities.  Caches are smaller, faster memory levels that store frequently accessed data. Effective cache utilization is crucial for performance optimization, as accessing data from cache is significantly faster than accessing main memory (DRAM).  Understanding cache behavior (e.g., cache lines, associativity, replacement policies) is essential for writing efficient programs.",
